          "ruleId": "CWE-116",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In json2html's object branch, line 86 `key = htmlEscape(key)` CLOBBERS the for-in loop variable, and the two subsequent statements then index the original object with the HTML-ESCAPED key: line 92 `isCollapsable(json[key])` and line 97 `json2html(json[key], options)`. For any attacker-supplied JSON object key containing one of & < > ' \" (e.g. {\"R&D\": \"secret\"}, {\"it's\": \"secret\"}, {\"a<b\": \"secret\"}), the escaped key (\"R&amp;D\") no longer names a property of `json`, so `json[key]` evaluates to undefined. `json2html(undefined, options)` then matches NONE of the dispatch branches -- typeof!=='string'/'number'/'boolean', `undefined === null` is false (strict), not `instanceof Array`, and line 80 is `typeof json === 'object'` which is false for undefined -- so it falls through the whole if/else chain and returns the initial `var html = ''` (line 42). Net effect: the viewer silently renders the key followed by ': ' and an EMPTY value, dropping the real value, and also suppresses the collapse toggle (isCollapsable(undefined) is false). Attacker control of a single key name therefore suppresses display of that key's value. Integrity/display-fidelity defect, not code execution -- it matters where this viewer is used to review security-relevant JSON (token scopes, permission objects, audit records), because a field's value can be made invisible to the reviewer. Note it does NOT throw, precisely because line 80 is a `typeof === 'object'` guard rather than a bare `else`. Fix: keep the raw key for indexing and escape only into the display string (e.g. `var keyRepr = htmlEscape(key)` without reassigning `key`).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source and confirmed. In json2html's object branch the loop variable is declared function-scoped at line 84 (`for (var key in json)`), then line 86 `key = htmlEscape(key)` overwrites that same binding. The only two subsequent uses of the key as an index -- line 92 `isCollapsable(json[key])` and line 97 `json2html(json[key], options)` -- therefore index the original object with the HTML-ESCAPED key. I read lines 84-104 in full: there is no saved copy of the raw key and no other assignment to `key`; `keyRepr` (line 87) is derived after the clobber so it cannot serve as the escape-for-display/raw-for-index split. htmlEscape (lines 29-35) rewrites & < > ' \" to entities, so for any key containing one of them (e.g. \"R&D\" -> \"R&amp;D\") `json[key]` is undefined. The fall-through is silent exactly as claimed: line 80 is a `typeof json === 'object'` guard rather than a bare `else`, so json2html(undefined) fails typeof string/number/boolean, fails `undefined === null` (strict), fails `instanceof Array`, and fails the typeof-object test, returning the initial `var html = ''` from line 42 with no exception; isCollapsable(undefined) is `undefined instanceof Object` -> false (line 13), suppressing the toggle. Reachability is solid, not a graph artifact: all calls in lines 84-99 carry reachable_from_entry=true, and 131 `json2html(json, options)` FLOWS_TO 137 `$(this).html(html)` off the sole public API `$.fn.jsonViewer` (line 118). Two corrections that strengthen the lead: (1) the impact is not only value-dropping but value SUBSTITUTION -- for {\"a&b\":1, \"a&amp;b\":2}, escaping \"a&b\" yields \"a&amp;b\" so json[\"a&amp;b\"] returns 2, making the row labelled a&b display the OTHER key's value, a controlled misattribution primitive; (2) the defect is structurally untested -- test/jquery.json-viewer.test.js:59-65 feeds the key '<script>alert(1)</script>' (which contains < and > and so triggers this bug) but asserts only that no <script> element exists, so the dropped value is never observed. Severity is LOW: the escaping works, so there is no XSS, code execution, or data exfiltration -- impact is confined to display fidelity, mattering only where the viewer reviews security-relevant JSON. CWE-116 is defensible but imprecise; the output encoding is correct and the real error is reusing the encoded value as a data-lookup key. Version pinned to jquery.json-viewer 1.4.0 (package.json:3); exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so there is no exploit-reference signal in either direction and I claim none."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "json-viewer/jquery.json-viewer.js",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
