        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1022",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Attacker-controlled JSON string values are rendered as links with `target=\"_blank\"` and NO `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`. At line 45 the string is htmlEscaped, then at line 47 `options.withLinks && isUrl(json)` gates line 48: `html += '<a href=\"' + json + '\" class=\"json-string\" target=\"_blank\">' + json + '</a>'`. `withLinks` DEFAULTS TO TRUE (line 124 `_tmp_6.withLinks = true`), so this is the out-of-the-box path. When the JSON string is an absolute http(s)/ftp(s) URL accepted by isUrl's regex, the newly opened attacker page receives a live `window.opener` handle to the hosting document and can navigate it (reverse tabnabbing / phishing redirect of the opener). Secondarily, isUrl's regex makes the scheme group OPTIONAL -- `(https?:\\/\\/|ftps?:\\/\\/)?` -- so a scheme-less string such as `evil.com` also matches and is emitted as `<a href=\"evil.com\">`, a RELATIVE URL resolved against the hosting page's base, which silently mis-renders the value as a same-origin link. I verified this is NOT an XSS: `\"` is escaped to `&quot;` by htmlEscape so the attribute cannot be broken out of (entity decoding happens after attribute tokenization), and the regex cannot match a `javascript:`/`data:` URI because the host-label class `[a-z0-9%-]` excludes `:`, the only other `:` positions are a digits-only port `(:(\\d{1,5}))?` and a `/`-prefixed path. Fix: add rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" and require an explicit safe scheme.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): The code defect is present exactly as claimed and I re-derived it from the real source. json-viewer/jquery.json-viewer.js:48 emits `<a href=\"' + json + '\" class=\"json-string\" target=\"_blank\">` with no `rel` attribute, and `withLinks` defaults to true (lines 120-125), so this is the default path; `json2html` (line 41) is an :EntryPoint with exposure=reachable and the line-48 call node is reachable_from_entry=true (hop_distance 1). test/jquery.json-viewer.test.js:16 asserts the rel-less anchor as the expected output, independently confirming this is committed, tested behavior rather than a transient state. I also independently confirmed the lead's negative claims: htmlEscape (lines 29-35) escapes `&` first and then `\"`->&quot;, so the attribute cannot be broken out of; and the isUrl regex (line 21) cannot yield a javascript:/data: href because the scheme alternation only offers https?://|ftps?:// and, when omitted, the mandatory group ([a-z0-9%-]+\\.){1,} must match from position 0 while `:` is absent from that class (so `javascript:` fails at its first non-class char). CWE-1022 is therefore the right classification and not XSS. Two corrections to the analyst: (1) the secondary scheme-less claim is weaker than stated -- `//evil.com` does NOT match either, since a leading `/` is not in the host class, so a bare `evil.com` produces only a relative same-origin href (a mis-render, not a cross-origin redirect); (2) severity is LOW, not MEDIUM, because the version is pinned at 1.4.0 (package.json:3) and all current browsers (Chrome 88+, Firefox 79+, Safari 12.1+, Edge 88+) apply an implicit rel=\"noopener\" to target=\"_blank\", nulling window.opener and removing the reverse-tabnabbing primitive the lead leads with. The impact that survives on every browser is the missing `noreferrer`: the full hosting-page URL (potentially containing tokens or IDs) leaks as Referer to an attacker-chosen destination, plus legacy-browser-only tabnabbing. The advisory exploit-reference check was unavailable (corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\" for both queries), so I claim neither that a weaponized exploit exists nor that this is patched."
          },
