        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1104",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Components-with-known-vulnerabilities / end-of-life toolchain: all three dev dependencies in this scan are pinned to majors that are past end-of-life and no longer receive security patches -- eslint \"^7.28.0\" (ESLint 7 is EOL; 8 is also now EOL, 9.x is current), jest \"^27.0.4\" (Jest 27 is unmaintained; 29/30 current), and eslint-plugin-jest \"^24.3.6\" (several majors behind, 28+ current). These are build/test-time only and are NOT attacker-reachable at runtime (no EntryPoint or CpgCall in the scan resolves into them), so runtime exploitability is nil; the exposure is supply-chain / developer-machine and CI, via their unpatched transitive trees (Jest 27 in particular pulls an old transitive graph). VERIFIER NOTE / EXPLICIT NON-CLAIM: I am NOT asserting any specific CVE -- the graph carries only name+version strings and I did not ground an advisory lookup this session. Confirm against the lockfile and an advisory database; this is an EOL/unmaintained-component lead.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): The factual substrate of the lead is real and I re-derived it from the manifest, not from the analyst. package.json:25-30 declares exactly four devDependencies, three of which are end-of-life/unmaintained toolchain majors: eslint ^7.28.0 (ESLint 7 dates to Jun 2020 and is unsupported; 9.x current), jest ^27.0.4 (May 2021, unmaintained; 29/30 current), eslint-plugin-jest ^24.3.6 (2021; 28+ current). They are used only by the \"lint\"/\"test\" scripts (package.json:22-23) and by CI (.github/workflows/node.js.yml:15-18, which additionally pins EOL node-version 14.x and actions/checkout@v2). This satisfies CWE-1104 (Use of Unmaintained Third-Party Components) on cited evidence, so I do not reject it.\n\nI independently confirmed the non-reachability claim: the CPG contains exactly one CpgFile (json-viewer/jquery.json-viewer.js), zero CpgModule nodes, zero CpgCalls whose code/method_full_name mention eslint or jest, and all four EntryPoints (htmlEscape, isCollapsable, isUrl, json2html) are inside that single plugin file. Source confirms this: the plugin is a self-contained browser IIFE with no imports, and only test/jquery.json-viewer.test.js:4-5 requires anything. Runtime attacker reachability is therefore nil.\n\nThree corrections / limits that cap this at LOW and keep it a hygiene finding rather than a vulnerability: (1) The analyst's transitive-tree sub-claim (\"unpatched transitive trees; Jest 27 pulls an old transitive graph\") is UNSUBSTANTIATED and I could not verify it. No lockfile is committed (.gitignore contains only \"node_modules\"; glob for package-lock.json / yarn.lock / pnpm-lock.yaml / npm-shrinkwrap.json found nothing) and node_modules is absent, so per the version-pinning rule the EXACT installed versions cannot be pinned -- only the major is fixed while the patch floats within an EOL range at install time. Exploitability is UNKNOWN; I assert no CVE and I do not claim any transitive package is either vulnerable or patched. mcp__orion__exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no advisory could be grounded in either direction. (2) \"all three dev dependencies in this scan\" is inaccurate -- there are four devDependencies; jquery ^3.6.0 (package.json:29) is also one and is past the 3.5.0 fix for the known jQuery htmlPrefilter XSS, so it correctly sits outside the EOL set. (3) The analyst gave no file/line at all, only \"CWE-1104\"; the location is package.json:25-30."
          },
